Tanasi Robert - 315CD
----------------------
For ease of implementation, i used a System struct which keeps
all of the relevant system information, which i passed to every
function that needed it as a pointer to avoid copying the data
every time.

For the purpose of recycling code, i kept every struct definition
as imposed by the problem statement, without a next / prev node,
as i have a separate, universal struct for that. It keeps a void*
pointer that i can then dereference as needed, and thus every
data structure required is implemented as a doubly linked circular
list with a sentinel node. I did not use the fictional incident
as a sentinel node as it would have required specific statements
(it isn't quite a sentinel node as its information can be requested
by the commands, like SHOW_INCIDENT). All of the functionality
for stacks and queues are implemented on top of these lists with
functions. For the available units' queue size, i kept a separate
variable as to not add an unnecessary variable to each node,
function, etc. and to avoid an O(N) solution for size by iterating.

For dynamic allocation and reallocation of char arrays, i used
an initial size of 16 (defined as a macro) and a reallocation
factor of 2x.

For freeing memory, i had to add two parameters to the free_list
function: want_free and is_incident, as the function is universal
except for two things: we have to avoid double frees, and make
sure to free the description when needed. (and it only exists 
for incidents).

All of the code is written by me, i only used ChatGPT to generate
an example of a char by char string reading algorithm which i
implemented myself after, not taking it as is. Otherwise, i used
it for some SEGFAULT / memory leak issues when i really couldn't
find / fix the problem with gdb and valgrind. 